1
00:00:00,560 --> 00:00:04,900
弗里曼: 我们能够理解外星人的思考方式吗？
Freeman: Do aliens think in ways we could ever understand?

2
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:08,700
所有形式的智能
Do all forms of intelligence

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:12,040
都有着同样的模式吗？
share the same underlying pattern --

4
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:16,550
一个我们才刚开始注意的模式？
a pattern we are only now beginning to see?

5
00:00:18,810 --> 00:00:22,150
通过研究地球上的非人生物,
By studying the nonhumans in our midst,

6
00:00:22,150 --> 00:00:25,720
科学家正在了解外星人的思维如何工作。
scientists are learning how alien minds function.

7
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,760
他们发现--
Their discovery --

8
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:32,160
情感可能比逻辑更为重要,
that emotions may be more important than logic,

9
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:35,630
就像脑对于身体的重要性一般。
and bodies matter as much as brains.

10
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:40,640
我们也许很快就会知道外星人如何思考。
We may soon know how aliens think.

11
00:00:46,370 --> 00:00:51,180
空间、时间、生命本身。
Space,time,life itself.

12
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:58,190
宇宙的奥秘，尽在穿越虫洞。
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

13
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:06,290
 外星人如何思考？

14
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:15,340
我们知道构成生命的基本元素已经遍布整个宇宙,
We know that the stuff of life is spread throughout the cosmos.

15
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:19,970
几率表明外星人无所不在。
Odds are that aliens are everywhere.

16
00:01:19,970 --> 00:01:23,210
外星人的思考方式和我们相同吗？
Will alien brains think like ours?

17
00:01:23,210 --> 00:01:26,580
还是他们可能就没有脑？
Will they have brains at all?

18
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:28,920
因为没有外星人可供研究,
Without an alien to study,

19
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:32,750
这个问题似乎没有答案。
these seem impossible questions to answer.

20
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:36,190
但是我们在这颗星球上并不孤独,
But we're not alone on this planet.

21
00:01:36,190 --> 00:01:39,690
如果想知道外星人如何思考,
If we want to know how aliens think,

22
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:43,200
我们可以从地球上那些与外星人思维接近的
we can start by looking closely at the alien minds

23
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,400
开始研究。
here on earth.

24
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:49,270
我曾经看着我的狗并想,
I used to look at my dog and wonder,

25
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,010
这里面到底在干嘛？
What's going on in there?

26
00:01:52,010 --> 00:01:54,810
她以我思考的方式思考吗？
did she think the way I did?

27
00:01:54,810 --> 00:01:59,610
至少我的狗看起来和我的世界有部分是一样的,
At least my dog seemed part of the same world as me.

28
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:03,520
而其它的是一些性质完全不同的生物,
Other creatures seemed utterly alien.

29
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,120
是否有可能
Is it possible

30
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:09,090
所有的生命都具有某些形式的智能,
that all life possesses some form of intelligence,

31
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:12,030
只是有时候和我们的差别太大
but sometimes so different from our own

32
00:02:12,030 --> 00:02:14,330
让我们没能发现吗？
that we fail to see it?

33
00:02:14,330 --> 00:02:20,070
是否有一种外星人思维就在我们眼前？
Could a form of alien thought be right under our noses?

34
00:02:20,070 --> 00:02:23,840
我们认为植物不能思考或感觉。
We believe plants cannot think or feel.

35
00:02:25,070 --> 00:02:27,380
但如果我们错了呢？
But what if we're wrong?

36
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:35,120
一个开始显现的新研究表明
A ground swell of new research suggests

37
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,750
植物拥有一种
plants possess a form of intelligence

38
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:40,890
和我们的完全不同的智能形式,
entirely alien to our own.

39
00:02:40,890 --> 00:02:45,030
大多数植物都可以嗅、尝、
Most plants can smell,taste,

40
00:02:45,030 --> 00:02:49,330
触,也许还有听,
touch,and perhaps hear,

41
00:02:49,930 --> 00:02:52,700
并且根据这两位研究者的观点,
and,according to these two researchers,

42
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:54,970
它们甚至会交谈。
they may even talk.

43
00:02:54,970 --> 00:02:58,570
Consuelo de Moraes拥有
Consuelo de Moraes has doctorates

44
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:01,140
化学和生物学博士学位,
in chemistry and biology.

45
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,880
Mark mescher的研究背景则是
Mark mescher's background is

46
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:07,350
在动物行为和进化方面。
in animal behavior and evolution.

47
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:09,820
这个二人组的实验证明
The duo's experiments have proven

48
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:11,820
植物可以表现出的行为方式
that plants behave in ways

49
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:16,590
我们通常只认为有脑的生物才可以做到。
we normally only attribute to creatures with brains.

50
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:18,290
我认为植物奇特的行为方式
I think what's alien about the way

51
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:20,860
就是它们会参与到自身环境中,
这对于作为动物的
plants engage their environments for us as animals

52
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:22,900
我们的直觉非常不一致。
is that it's just so counterintuitive.

53
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:24,970
动物,尤其是人类,
Animals,and particularly humans,

54
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:26,670
典型的以视觉为导向,
we're very visually oriented.

55
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:28,510
以认知为导向。
We're very cognitively oriented.

56
00:03:28,510 --> 00:03:30,110
我们可以解决很多问题
We're these problem solvers

57
00:03:30,110 --> 00:03:32,280
是因为某些程度的面对自身环境
that are used to sort of encountering our environments

58
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:33,440
并对其进行思考
and thinking about them

59
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:35,440
以想出许多解决办法。
and coming up with ways to solve problems.

60
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:38,410
植物也可以解决问题,却以非常不同的方式
And plants are solving problems,but in a very different way

61
00:03:38,410 --> 00:03:40,450
与它们的环境相互作用,
of interacting with their environment.

62
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:43,920
弗里曼：最近,Mark和Consuelo
Freeman: Recently,Mark and Consuelo

63
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,420
使用了信息素检测仪器
have used pheromone detecting machines

64
00:03:46,420 --> 00:03:50,390
和延时摄像机来研究一种无叶寄生藤,
and time-lapse cameras to study a leafless parasitic vine

65
00:03:50,390 --> 00:03:53,730
叫做五角菟丝子。
called Cuscuta pentagona.

66
00:03:53,730 --> 00:03:56,970
菟丝子表现出了很多的解决问题行为,
Cuscuta exhibits a host of problem-solving behaviors

67
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:59,900
这些我们从未期待能在植物中发现。
we don't expect to see in plants.

68
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:04,270
但是这个令人着迷的生命形式却有着阴暗面。
But this fascinating life form has a dark side.

69
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:08,680
它看起来像是普通藤蔓,
It may look like an ordinary vine,

70
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:13,820
但在植物界,它是恶毒的连环杀手。
but in the plant world,it's a vicious serial killer.

71
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:15,990
De Moraes: 菟丝子是完全寄生的,
De Moraes: Cuscuta is a true parasite,

72
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:19,960
和大多数植物不同,它们没有根。
so as opposed to most plants,they don't have roots.

73
00:04:19,960 --> 00:04:22,260
它们不需要光合作用,
They don't really do photosynthesis,

74
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:23,690
那么为了生存,
so in order to survive,

75
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:26,030
它们就必须附着在宿主植物上,
they must attach to a host plant.

76
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:28,870
它们某种意义上就像吸血鬼。
They work like vampires,in a sense.

77
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:31,100
它们可以吸取植物体内的资源,
They will take the resources out of the plant

78
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:33,540
然后基于这些从宿主植物中
and then grow based on the resources

79
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:34,840
得到的营养物质生长,
from the host plant

80
00:04:34,840 --> 00:04:36,640
所以它们不会产生任何东西。
so they will not produce anything.

81
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:38,540
它们是真正的寄生生物。
They're true parasites.

82
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,180
弗里曼：随着菟丝子藤的生长,
Freeman: As a cuscuta vine grows,

83
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:46,520
它可以像我们在午夜时分用手摸索
it probes its surroundings the way we do with our hands

84
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:48,690
洗手间灯的开关一样
when we search for the bathroom light

85
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:50,190
探测周围环境。
in the middle of the night.

86
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:55,160
藤蔓会弯曲并生长,直到它找到了美味的东西--
The vine bends and grows until it finds something tasty --

87
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:57,990
比如一株西红柿,
say,a tomato plant.

88
00:04:57,990 --> 00:05:00,230
接着它向下摸索
Then it probes downward

89
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:02,870
直至找到枝干的根基,
until it finds the base of the stem,

90
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:06,470
在这里它插入了自己的獠牙。
which it sinks its teeth into.

91
00:05:06,470 --> 00:05:11,740
它会用自身将宿主缠绕起来,并吸取宿主珍贵的汁液,
It wraps itself around the plant and sucks out its vital juices,

92
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:15,910
非常神奇的是,菟丝子可以闻出
and strangely enough,cuscuta can smell

93
00:05:15,910 --> 00:05:18,920
它的目标是否健康。
whether its potential victims are healthy or not.

94
00:05:18,920 --> 00:05:21,900
De Moraes：我们接受动物可以
De Moraes: We accept that animals

95
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,890
依赖嗅觉找到宿主。
can find the host based on smell.

96
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:27,360
我们总是能看到,对吧？
We see that all the time,right?

97
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:30,860
但我们就是不认为植物也拥有这种能力,
It's just we don't think that plants have that ability,

98
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:33,560
而我觉得这十分迷人,
and I think that's what's fascinating,

99
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:37,700
因为我们刚刚越过我们自己划分出的
because we just have this line that we have drawn

100
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:42,940
动物能做得到而植物不能的界线。
that says animals can do this but plants cannot.

101
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:48,450
弗里曼：植物也能够通过嗅觉交流,
Freeman: Plants can also use smell to communicate.

102
00:05:48,450 --> 00:05:52,080
有些植物使用气味防御捕食者
Some use odors to defend themselves against predators

103
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,450
并互相警告危险的存在。
and warn each other of danger.

104
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:56,850
举个例子,毛虫正在吃这颗
For instance,caterpillars are eating the leaves

105
00:05:56,950 --> 00:05:58,760
大豆植物的叶片,
of this soybean plant.

106
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:01,760
大豆植物就会通过释放
The plant calls for help

107
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:05,300
能够吸引胡蜂的化学物质来获得帮助,
by releasing chemicals that attract wasps.

108
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:09,630
胡蜂会把毛虫蛰死。
The wasps sting the caterpillars to death.

109
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:12,340
当这一切正在发生时,
While this is going on,

110
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:15,340
其他植物闻到了胁迫信号
other plants smell the distress signal

111
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:19,140
就会释放它们自己的化学物质抵御攻击。
and release their own chemicals to ward off attack.

112
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:23,580
植物的生存周期不同于人类,
Plants live on a different time scale from humans.

113
00:06:23,580 --> 00:06:26,520
它们的行为表现得非常缓慢,
Their actions unfold so slowly,

114
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:29,350
以至于我们意识不到一切正在发生。
we think nothing is happening at all.

115
00:06:29,350 --> 00:06:33,520
实际上植物可以自我防御,与其他植物交流,
But plants defend themselves,communicate with other plants,

116
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:40,300
通过气味识别受伤的邻居、寻找食物。
recognize injured neighbors by scent,and sniff out meals.

117
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:44,930
植物比人类出现得早,
Plants predate the human race.

118
00:06:44,930 --> 00:06:49,070
它们存在了很长的时间,
They may well outlast it,so is it likely

119
00:06:49,070 --> 00:06:53,440
那么它们这种另类的思维和行为
that their very alien form of thought and behavior

120
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:57,050
会存在于其他世界中吗？
could be found on other worlds?

121
00:06:57,050 --> 00:06:59,020
Mescher: 不同植物种类的进化史
Mescher: The history of evolution on different planets

122
00:06:59,020 --> 00:07:00,650
差异都是很大的。
may unfold in very different ways.

123
00:07:00,650 --> 00:07:03,290
我认为我们很可能能够找到
I think we would be very likely to find something

124
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:06,120
与植物行为类似的生态系统,
that was very similar ecologically to what plants do.

125
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,090
我认为它们也能够基本上从恒星获得能量,
I think they're able to take energy from starlight,

126
00:07:08,090 --> 00:07:09,360
并转化为
basically,and convert that

127
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:12,100
它们自身可以利用的化学能。
into biochemical energy that they can then use.

128
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:13,730
这正是食物链的基础,
That's just the base of the food chain,

129
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:15,800
就算是在其他星球上,这就是符合逻辑的
and it seems that that would be the logical place

130
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,240
生态系统的起始点。
for an ecosystem to start,even on another planet.

131
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:23,510
弗里曼：问题是其他星球上的植物
Freeman: The question is are plants on other worlds

132
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:27,380
会比我们世界中的善于思考吗？
any better at thinking than the ones we know?

133
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:31,110
外星树木会用枝干思索吗？
Do alien trees contemplate their branches?

134
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:36,120
外星花朵会有对没有阳光的世界的噩梦吗？
Do alien flowers have nightmares about worlds with no sun?

135
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:38,750
也许外星植物已经发展出了
Perhaps alien plants have developed

136
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:43,860
复杂的思维,变得拥有自我意识。
the complexity of thought it takes to become self-aware.

137
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,470
但这个人可能会说,“不会的”,
This woman would say “No.”

138
00:07:48,470 --> 00:07:53,440
她认为不论地球还是外太空的生命形式
she believes that no life form here on earth or out in space

139
00:07:53,440 --> 00:07:58,710
只要没有移动的能力就不会进化出复杂的思维,
can evolve complex thought until it has the ability to move

140
00:07:58,710 --> 00:08:03,580
这样也无法通过移动感知世界。
and to sense the world as it moves through it.

141
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:06,750
对于认知科学家Saskia Nagel来说,
For cognitive scientist Saskia Nagel,

142
00:08:06,750 --> 00:08:10,550
感觉输入和身体运动的相互影响
the interplay of sensory input and bodily motion

143
00:08:10,550 --> 00:08:15,760
是意识出现的基础。
is the foundation upon which consciousness is built.

144
00:08:15,760 --> 00:08:18,060
比如说,我看到这个杯子,
For instance,I see this cup,

145
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:20,730
我能够用手指感觉它,
I can feel this cup with my fingers,

146
00:08:20,730 --> 00:08:22,630
同样如果我拿起它也能感觉到
and also,I can sense the weight of this cup

147
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:23,670
它的重量。
if I grab it.

148
00:08:23,670 --> 00:08:25,100
这就是感觉输入。
That's sensory input.

149
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:27,740
但这同时又是紧密耦合的,
But at the same time,closely coupled,

150
00:08:27,740 --> 00:08:29,910
当我观察的时候眼球可以转动,
my eyes move when I look at it,

151
00:08:29,910 --> 00:08:32,310
我要抓住什么东西时就要移动手臂,
I have to move my arm to grab it,

152
00:08:32,310 --> 00:08:34,380
如果我想感觉到它的重量
and I have to keep it like this

153
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:36,610
就必须这样行动,
if I want to sense the weight of it,

154
00:08:36,610 --> 00:08:38,580
我们认为偶联
and we think that the coupling

155
00:08:38,580 --> 00:08:41,320
感觉过程和行动过程
of sensory processes and motor processes

156
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:43,450
才允许我们感知世界。
is what allows us to perceive.

157
00:08:43,450 --> 00:08:46,820
弗里曼：我们对于什么才是意识的想法
Freeman: Our idea of what it means to be conscious

158
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:51,300
离不开我们人类特有的感受--
is inextricably tied to our specific human senses --

159
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:57,100
看、闻、听、触和尝的能力。
the ability to see,smell,hear,touch,and taste.

160
00:08:57,100 --> 00:09:01,110
但Saskia认为外星人可能发展出了丰富的精神生活
But Saskia believes that aliens could develop rich mental lives

161
00:09:01,110 --> 00:09:05,510
即使他们以完全不同的方式感受世界。
even if they sense their world in entirely different ways.

162
00:09:07,750 --> 00:09:10,980
为了证明这一观点,她把外星人的感受
To prove it,she's feeding an alien sense

163
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:14,050
灌输到一组志愿者的神经系统中,
into the nervous system of a group of volunteers.

164
00:09:14,050 --> 00:09:18,590
这要让他们穿上称为空间感觉腰带的设备,
By wearing a device called a feelspace belt,

165
00:09:18,590 --> 00:09:20,620
受试者正在学习
test subjects are learning

166
00:09:20,630 --> 00:09:24,000
如何去感觉地磁北极的方向,
how to feel the position of magnetic north.

167
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,870
这就给了他们完美的方向感。
This gives them a perfect sense of direction.

168
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:31,070
这就是个装有一些震动器的腰带,
It's just a belt equipped with a number of vibrators.

169
00:09:31,070 --> 00:09:33,600
在背面有一个小罗盘--
On the back side,there's a little compass --

170
00:09:33,610 --> 00:09:35,170
实际上是个非常好的罗盘--
a very good one,actually --

171
00:09:35,170 --> 00:09:38,440
在另一侧那边还有一个微型电脑,
and a little computer at the other side down there,

172
00:09:38,440 --> 00:09:41,950
罗盘和计算机可以指示
and the compass and the computer always tell one element

173
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:45,820
这些震动器中的一个在适当的时候震动,
of these vibration elements at the time to vibrate,

174
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:48,950
通常都是指北的震荡器。
and it's always that element that points north.

175
00:09:48,950 --> 00:09:52,490
弗里曼：今天,Saskia和她的研究小组
Freeman: Today,Saskia and her team

176
00:09:52,490 --> 00:09:56,160
让三名志愿者来到草坪上,
have taken three volunteers into a forest.

177
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:01,270
志愿者戴着完全漆黑的护目镜。
The volunteers put on blacked-out goggles.

178
00:10:01,270 --> 00:10:05,940
接着转动他们直到无法分清方向。
Next,they are spun around until they are disoriented.

179
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:09,710
现在他们必须找到到达Saskia的方向,
Now they have to find their way to Saskia,

180
00:10:09,710 --> 00:10:13,850
她正站在准确的正北方向。
who stands at a fixed position directly north.

181
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:17,850
通常情况下他们是不可能找到的,
Ordinarily,they wouldn't stand a chance,

182
00:10:17,850 --> 00:10:20,380
但每个志愿者都花了六周的时间
but each volunteer has spent six weeks

183
00:10:20,390 --> 00:10:22,290
训练如何使用腰带。
training with the belt.

184
00:10:22,290 --> 00:10:26,890
他们已经形成了内在的罗盘。
They have developed a built-in compass.

185
00:10:26,890 --> 00:10:30,860
Nagel: 实际上,有趣的是他们在训练之后
Nagel: Actually,it's interesting that they don't feel

186
00:10:30,860 --> 00:10:34,500
就感觉不到也不需要震动刺激了,
the tactile stimulation anymore after the training.

187
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:38,900
就好像这种新感觉总是存在了,
It is as if this new sense has always been there,

188
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:43,140
他们的确形成了另一种空间感。
and they really develop a different sense of space.

189
00:10:43,140 --> 00:10:45,810
弗里曼：距离何时我们才能有
Freeman: The day when we have solid evidence

190
00:10:45,810 --> 00:10:49,380
外星人如何思考的确切证据还很远,
of how aliens think may be a long way off,

191
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:54,020
但这样的实验给了我们方法来估计
but experiments like this give us a way of approximating

192
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:56,390
以不同方式感知世界的
what it's like in alien heads

193
00:10:56,390 --> 00:10:59,690
外星人的头脑会是什么样。
with an alien way of sensing the world.

194
00:10:59,690 --> 00:11:02,030
结果表明思考的能力
They show us the ability to think

195
00:11:02,030 --> 00:11:05,600
并不依赖于我们已知的感觉。
is not dependent on the senses we know.

196
00:11:09,630 --> 00:11:12,570
逻辑上来讲,外星人会进化出
Logically,aliens would evolve senses

197
00:11:12,570 --> 00:11:15,670
与自己所处环境相符合的感觉。
tailored to their own particular environments.

198
00:11:15,670 --> 00:11:19,980
在一个暗淡无光的星球上,拥有内在的雷达系统
On a planet where light is dim,having a built-in radar system

199
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:22,710
会比拥有视觉更有价值。
would be more valuable than the sense of sight.

200
00:11:22,710 --> 00:11:27,520
外星人可能有着完全不同的一套感知系统
An alien could have a completely different set of senses

201
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:31,120
并始终在他们自己的世界中占着优势。
and still dominate its world.

202
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:34,560
那么一群外星人如何思考呢？
But how do groups of aliens think?

203
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:40,370
他们如何互相交流、组织,
How do they communicate with each other,organize,

204
00:11:40,370 --> 00:11:42,940
来形成精密复杂的社会？
and form sophisticated societies?

205
00:11:45,540 --> 00:11:49,780
答案也许就在脚下。
The answer may be right under our feet.

206
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:58,040
你的脑就像曲折交错的铁轨迷宫,
Your brain is like a maze of twisting railroad tracks.

207
00:11:58,040 --> 00:12:01,710
随着神经元的生长,它们可以产生数万亿个联结,
As neurons grow,they make trillions of connections,

208
00:12:01,710 --> 00:12:04,780
通过这些难以置信的复杂网络,
and out of this incredibly complex network

209
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:08,480
你的思维奇迹般的诞生了。
arises the miracle of your mind.

210
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:10,850
但谁能肯定其他星球上的
But who's to say it works the same way

211
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:13,220
智慧生命用同样的方式运作呢？
for intelligent life on other planets?

212
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:16,650
如果外星人脑部的神经连接
What if the neural connections of alien brains

213
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:20,690
可以扩散到不同的个体呢？
are spread across many different bodies?

214
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:27,300
Nigel Franks教授正在寻找答案,
Professor Nigel Franks is looking for an answer

215
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:32,640
通过研究地球上最常见的生命形式--
by closely studying the dominant form of life on earth --

216
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:34,640
昆虫。
insects.

217
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:36,570
我专业研究蚂蚁
So,I've been working on ants professionally now

218
00:12:36,570 --> 00:12:38,180
至今有30年了,
for about 30 years,

219
00:12:38,180 --> 00:12:40,450
每天我们都有成功的实验。
and every day,we have a successful experiment.

220
00:12:40,450 --> 00:12:41,810
它们使我惊讶,带给我乐趣,
They surprise and delight me,

221
00:12:41,810 --> 00:12:44,220
我认为让我一心研究它们的是
and I think the thing that's absolutely riveted me

222
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:47,480
它们比我们曾经认为的
is now we know that ants are so much more sophisticated

223
00:12:47,490 --> 00:12:49,750
更加复杂。
than we ever knew them to be.

224
00:12:49,750 --> 00:12:52,160
弗里曼: 当蚂蚁聚集起来,
Freeman: When ants get together,

225
00:12:52,160 --> 00:12:57,230
它们形成了一个超个体--一个由微小、并不聪明的部分
they form a superorganism -- a large,intelligent life form

226
00:12:57,230 --> 00:13:00,770
组成的庞大、智慧的生命形式。
made of small,not especially intelligent parts.

227
00:13:00,770 --> 00:13:03,070
Franks：我认为奇特的是
Franks: What's special,I think,

228
00:13:03,070 --> 00:13:05,600
蚂蚁互相交流的方式,
is that the way the ants communicate with one another,

229
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:08,470
整个群体高效的聚集,
so the whole colony emerges effectively

230
00:13:08,470 --> 00:13:10,310
就像脑部结构。
as a brain-like structure.

231
00:13:14,050 --> 00:13:16,820
弗里曼：为了观察这个聚集脑的行为,
Freeman: To see this mass brain in action,

232
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:20,020
Nigel 让蚂蚁们进行室内狩猎。
Nigel forces the ants to go house hunting.

233
00:13:20,020 --> 00:13:22,320
Franks: 是的,这个实验中,
Franks: Right,so,in this experiment,

234
00:13:22,320 --> 00:13:24,390
我们取了一个蚂蚁集落的部分蚂蚁,
we've taken one of our ant colonies.

235
00:13:24,390 --> 00:13:26,720
对于他们来说我们真的很坏,因为我们彻底摧毁了
We've been really mean to them,because we've totally destroyed

236
00:13:26,720 --> 00:13:29,130
它们曾经居住的巢穴,
the nest that they've been living in,

237
00:13:29,130 --> 00:13:32,030
而这意味着他们需要找一个新家,
and what that means is they have to find a new home,

238
00:13:32,030 --> 00:13:35,600
我们给它们提供两个巢穴以供选择,
and we've offered them a choice of two alternative nest sites.

239
00:13:35,600 --> 00:13:36,870
这里的这个
There's one over here,

240
00:13:36,870 --> 00:13:38,940
过于明亮,而且入口很宽,
which is too light and got a very wide entrance,

241
00:13:38,940 --> 00:13:41,540
这两点都是它们不喜欢的,
which is two things they don't like,

242
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:43,570
相比起来它们会更喜欢这个巢穴,
versus this nest that they really should prefer

243
00:13:43,570 --> 00:13:45,910
因为它有着非常漂亮的狭窄入口,
because it's got a beautiful narrow entrance.

244
00:13:45,910 --> 00:13:48,880
里面有足够大的空间居住,
It's got plenty of space for them to live in,

245
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:52,020
这张红色滤光片意味着巢穴内是黑暗的,
and this red filter means that the nest is dark,

246
00:13:52,020 --> 00:13:54,090
这些是它们喜欢的,
and that's something they prefer,as well,

247
00:13:54,090 --> 00:13:56,790
我们就期望它们能够选择这个巢穴,
so we would expect them to be able to choose this nest.

248
00:13:56,790 --> 00:13:59,460
而这个实验的有趣之处就是要
And the beauty of this experiment is we should be able

249
00:13:59,460 --> 00:14:01,230
弄明白它们到底如何
to work out exactly how they go about

250
00:14:01,230 --> 00:14:03,060
做出这样的决定的。
making this kind of decision.

251
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:05,960
弗里曼：蚂蚁是挑剔的购物者,
Freeman: Ants are picky shoppers.

252
00:14:05,960 --> 00:14:08,670
当他们估计一个新家时,
When they size up a new home,

253
00:14:08,670 --> 00:14:11,270
会仔细考虑天花板的高度,
they carefully consider the height of the ceilings,

254
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:14,040
占地面积,房间宽度,
the floor space,the width of the rooms,

255
00:14:14,040 --> 00:14:16,440
以及入口数量,
and the number of entrances.

256
00:14:16,440 --> 00:14:19,680
它们还会排斥有卫生问题的巢穴,
They will reject nests that have hygiene problems,

257
00:14:19,680 --> 00:14:24,110
比如还有上个房客残留。
such as the remains of a previous tenant.

258
00:14:24,120 --> 00:14:26,580
[尖叫]
[ Screams ]

259
00:14:26,580 --> 00:14:28,850
Franks：如果蚂蚁喜欢某个巢穴,
Franks: If an ant likes a particular nest,

260
00:14:28,850 --> 00:14:33,260
它会回到群体那里找一个可以接受新巢穴的同伴,
it goes home to the colony and finds a receptive nest mate,

261
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:36,390
它就带着它来到这里,
and it leads it back to that nest site.

262
00:14:36,390 --> 00:14:39,730
基本上领头的蚂蚁会告诉跟着它的蚂蚁
Basically,the leading ant teaches a single following ant

263
00:14:39,730 --> 00:14:41,630
往新巢穴怎么走,
the route to the new nest site,

264
00:14:41,630 --> 00:14:44,970
接着如果它也喜欢这里,也会回家去招募更多蚂蚁来。
then,if it likes it,as well,go home and recruit yet more.

265
00:14:44,970 --> 00:14:48,770
这样1只蚂蚁成为2只、4只、8只、16只,
So 1 ant becomes 2 becomes 4 becomes 8 becomes 16,

266
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:51,210
当它们在新巢穴拥有
and when they build a sufficient number of their nest mates

267
00:14:51,210 --> 00:14:52,380
足够数量的同伴,
in the new nest site,

268
00:14:52,380 --> 00:14:55,080
它们就实现了我们称作的法定人数阈值,
they achieve what we call a quorum threshold,

269
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:57,580
这就是它们决定搬去
and that's the decision point that makes them commit

270
00:14:57,580 --> 00:14:59,280
新巢穴的转折点,
to that particular nest site,

271
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:01,620
接着它们开始携带着同伴去新家,
and then they'll start carrying their nest mates there

272
00:15:01,620 --> 00:15:02,720
而不是引导它们。
rather than leading them.

273
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:10,060
弗里曼：最近,计算机科学家James Marshall
Freeman: Recently,computer scientist James Marshall

274
00:15:10,060 --> 00:15:13,200
使用Nigel的发现建立了一个蚂蚁决策的
used Nigel's findings to create a sophisticated model

275
00:15:13,200 --> 00:15:15,000
精密模型。
of ant decision-making.

276
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,340
接着有些引人注目的事情发生了。
Then something remarkable happened.

277
00:15:18,340 --> 00:15:21,710
James还进行着另一项计算机模拟程序,
James came upon another computer simulation

278
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:25,710
和他正在研究的很相似。
that looked very much like the one he was working on.

279
00:15:25,710 --> 00:15:28,380
一个同事加入了我在普林斯顿的研究部门,
A colleague came to join my department from Princeton,

280
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:30,450
他正在研究的是
where he'd been working on modeling

281
00:15:30,450 --> 00:15:32,380
灵长类脑部的决策回路,
primate decision-making circuits in the brain

282
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:34,650
这基本和我开始制作这些蚂蚁决策制定的
just around the same time I started making

283
00:15:34,650 --> 00:15:35,820
计算机和数学模型
these computer and math models

284
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:38,720
同时开始,
of ant colonies making decisions,

285
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:40,260
我们互相拜访讨论,
and we went to each other's talks,

286
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:42,430
都对我们的发现感到非常兴奋,
and we were both really excited because we could see

287
00:15:42,430 --> 00:15:44,600
因为这两个模型看起来像是同一类系统。
that each was basically looking at the same kind of system.

288
00:15:44,600 --> 00:15:47,230
尽管一个是蚂蚁集落,一个是灵长类脑,
Although one was an ant colony and one was a primate brain,

289
00:15:47,230 --> 00:15:49,470
它们都基本上以同样的方式
they were both basically working in the same way

290
00:15:49,470 --> 00:15:50,370
作出决策。
to make decisions.

291
00:15:50,370 --> 00:15:54,540
弗里曼：James意识到
Freeman: James realized

292
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:56,910
超个体中的蚂蚁个体的行为
that individual ants in a superorganism

293
00:15:56,910 --> 00:16:01,610
类似于灵长类脑中的单个神经元。
behave like the neurons in a single primate brain.

294
00:16:01,610 --> 00:16:04,880
Marshall: 我认为蚂蚁以一种非常有趣的方式
Marshall: So,I think ants collectively process information

295
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:06,380
共同处理信息。
in a very interesting way.

296
00:16:06,380 --> 00:16:08,120
蚂蚁个体并不需要知道
No individual ant needs to know

297
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:10,450
关于它周围环境的所有细节,
all the details about its environment,

298
00:16:10,450 --> 00:16:12,920
但通过只获得一点信息
but by having a little bit of information

299
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:15,760
并将信息聚集到群体水平,
and aggregating it together at the colony level,

300
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:18,830
群体就能够做出明智的决定,
the colony can be well informed about the decision it's making,

301
00:16:18,830 --> 00:16:20,860
即使蚂蚁个体并不聪明,
even if individual ants are less well informed,

302
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:23,300
这类似于脑中的神经元。
and that's analogous to neurons in the brain.

303
00:16:23,300 --> 00:16:25,100
单个神经元并不能知道什么,
No individual neuron really knows anything,

304
00:16:25,100 --> 00:16:26,740
但许多神经元聚集起来形成脑,
but you stick a load of them together,and collectively,

305
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:30,140
就知道了很多事情。
the brain does know something.

306
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:34,780
弗里曼：那么给其足够的时间,
Freeman: So,given enough time,

307
00:16:34,780 --> 00:16:38,720
蚂蚁群体会变得有意识吗？
could ant colonies become conscious?

308
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:41,450
或是它们已经有了意识？
Are they already conscious?

309
00:16:41,450 --> 00:16:43,690
Franks: 我认为在我们解决问题的方式
Franks: Well,I think one of the great differences

310
00:16:43,690 --> 00:16:45,590
和蚂蚁解决问题的方式之间
between the way we solve problems

311
00:16:45,590 --> 00:16:46,920
存在着明显不同,
and the way ants solve problems

312
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:48,990
这就是蚂蚁必须面对问题
is the ants actually have to mingle with the problem

313
00:16:48,990 --> 00:16:50,730
才开始去解决它,
to begin to solve it.

314
00:16:50,730 --> 00:16:52,960
而我们能做的是
And the great difference we have is that we can

315
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:55,130
在我们的脑袋中制造虚拟现实
often play virtual-reality games in our heads

316
00:16:55,130 --> 00:16:57,130
考虑另一种选择的情况,
to think about alternative scenarios

317
00:16:57,130 --> 00:16:59,200
以及我们会如何解决问题
and how we might solve the problem

318
00:16:59,200 --> 00:17:01,770
和这样决定的后果。
and what the consequences of that might be.

319
00:17:01,770 --> 00:17:04,210
那我们做的是先去进行思考,
So,what we do is stand back and think about things,

320
00:17:04,210 --> 00:17:06,240
而蚂蚁们则是卷起袖子
whereas the ants really roll up their sleeves

321
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:07,410
马上着手去干。
and get on with it.

322
00:17:07,410 --> 00:17:10,850
弗里曼：蚁群能够完成的工作
Freeman: Ant colonies can accomplish goals

323
00:17:10,850 --> 00:17:14,920
远超过了个体成员的能力。
far beyond the capabilities of their individual members.

324
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:16,820
但它们只能思考
But they can only think about things

325
00:17:16,820 --> 00:17:19,560
摆在它们面前的事。
that are directly in front of them.

326
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:24,800
它们缺少自我意识和想象的能力,
They lack self awareness and the ability to imagine.

327
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:28,030
或许外星昆虫拥有这些能力,
Perhaps alien insects have these abilities.

328
00:17:28,030 --> 00:17:32,540
只是在地球上,它们不能。
On earth,they do not.

329
00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:35,170
那么地球上是否有非人智慧
So,is there a nonhuman intelligence on earth

330
00:17:35,170 --> 00:17:39,710
能够与我们的思想和创新匹敌吗？
that rivals us for thoughtfulness and innovation?

331
00:17:39,710 --> 00:17:43,010
这个人说有。
This man says yes.

332
00:17:43,010 --> 00:17:46,650
他正在研究的生物可能就能解开
He's studying a creature that may hold the secret

333
00:17:46,650 --> 00:17:51,520
外星人思维和意识的奥秘。
to alien thought and the mystery of consciousness.

334
00:17:54,990 --> 00:17:57,860
人类的脑在地球上不是最大的,
Human brains are not the largest on earth,

335
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:01,230
但却是最复杂的。
but they are the most complex.

336
00:18:01,230 --> 00:18:04,200
我们的脑不仅可以思考,
Our brains don't just think.

337
00:18:04,200 --> 00:18:06,970
也有自我意识。
They also are self-aware.

338
00:18:06,970 --> 00:18:09,970
它们是有意识的,
They are conscious.

339
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:13,250
意识只存在于人脑中吗,
Is consciousness unique to the human brain

340
00:18:13,250 --> 00:18:15,510
还是拥有着完全不同的
or could a creature with radically different

341
00:18:15,510 --> 00:18:19,150
脑部结构的生物也具有意识？
brain architecture also be self-aware?

342
00:18:21,190 --> 00:18:26,820
答案就隐藏在这些不会眨动的眼睛后面。
The answers may lurk behind these unblinking eyes.

343
00:18:26,830 --> 00:18:29,900
章鱼是无脊椎动物,
Octopuses are invertebrates.

344
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:31,560
它们没有脊髓,
They don't have spinal cords,

345
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:34,600
但仍表现出较高水平的智力,
yet they exhibit a level of intelligence

346
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:38,440
而我们通常只认为哺乳动物才具有。
we usually associate only with mammals.

347
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:44,380
它们似乎具有思维,很聪明,甚至会计算,
They appear to be thoughtful,clever,even calculating,

348
00:18:44,380 --> 00:18:48,750
但它们与我们完全不同。
but they are utterly unlike us.

349
00:18:48,750 --> 00:18:52,250
章鱼是真正的非常接近
Octopuses are truly the closest thing

350
00:18:52,250 --> 00:18:57,020
外星生命形式的、具有某种程度智慧的
to an alien life form of any degree of intelligence

351
00:18:57,020 --> 00:18:59,660
地球生物。
on the planet earth.

352
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:02,160
弗里曼：神经科学家David Edelman
Freeman: Neuroscientist David Edelman studies

353
00:19:02,160 --> 00:19:07,200
研究章鱼的脑,更准确地说是许多脑,
the brain of the octopus,or more precisely,brains.

354
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:09,970
它不仅有一个脑。
It has more than one.

355
00:19:09,970 --> 00:19:13,200
普通章鱼有5亿个神经元或神经细胞,
In the common octopus,we have half a billion neurons,

356
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:16,970
一半以上都位于它的触手中,
or nerve cells,more than half of which are in the arms,

357
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:20,610
这些触手非常非常有趣,
and those arms,in fact,are really,really interesting

358
00:19:20,610 --> 00:19:22,920
因为你可以把它们看做
because you can almost characterize them

359
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:26,850
拥有属于自己的微型脑的触手。
as having their own miniature brain.

360
00:19:29,360 --> 00:19:32,220
弗里曼：章鱼的多重脑
Freeman: The multiple brains of the octopus

361
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:34,960
像计算机的分布式处理系统一样工作--
act as a distributed processing system --

362
00:19:34,960 --> 00:19:38,500
触手传送信息到头部,
the arms feeding data to the head

363
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:43,000
就像计算机节点将信息传送到中央集线器,
like a network of computer nodes feed data to a central hub.

364
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:47,140
如果切断其中一个节点,它仍能继续工作。
If one of the nodes is cut off,it can still function.

365
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:50,410
Edelman: 如果你切掉了一个章鱼的触手,
Edelman: If you cut off an octopus's arm,

366
00:19:50,410 --> 00:19:54,310
被切下的触手仍能进行一些行为,
that arm is actually able to execute a series of movements

367
00:19:54,310 --> 00:19:57,020
这些行为非常非常协调,这十分有趣,
that are very,very coordinated,which is really intriguing,

368
00:19:57,020 --> 00:20:00,320
在脊椎动物中你无法看到这类现象。
and you do not see that at all in any vertebrate.

369
00:20:00,320 --> 00:20:03,020
[触手抱怨]
[ Arm murmuring ]

370
00:20:05,020 --> 00:20:06,730
弗里曼: 你如何能够知道
Freeman: How do you work out

371
00:20:06,730 --> 00:20:10,230
拥有另类脑的生物是如何思考的？
what a creature with an alien brain is thinking?

372
00:20:10,230 --> 00:20:13,970
David的办法是对章鱼使用一些
David's solution was to adapt intelligence tests

373
00:20:13,970 --> 00:20:18,240
改编自鼠类的智力测试。
made for mice and rats to octopuses.

374
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:20,840
你看到的是巴恩斯迷宫,
So,what you see here is a Barnes maze,

375
00:20:20,840 --> 00:20:24,680
起初是设计用来研究啮齿类动物的
which was originally designed for looking at memory

376
00:20:24,680 --> 00:20:29,380
记忆学习和实际视觉导航,
and learning and actual visual navigation in rodents,

377
00:20:29,380 --> 00:20:31,480
我们在这里做的是
and what we're doing here is we're checking

378
00:20:31,480 --> 00:20:35,520
测试章鱼的视觉导航能力,
the octopus's capability for visual navigation,

379
00:20:35,520 --> 00:20:38,890
你看到的是一个18个洞的
and so what you see here is this fairly simple maze

380
00:20:38,890 --> 00:20:42,760
非常简单的迷宫,但只有一个洞是真洞,
with 18 holes,but only one hole is a true hole

381
00:20:42,760 --> 00:20:44,630
它可以从中逃脱,
that leads to an escape.

382
00:20:44,630 --> 00:20:46,630
逃到装有海水的容器中。
The escape is into a container of its own seawater.

383
00:20:46,630 --> 00:20:50,470
你能发现这有一些视觉标志,
But you also see these visual landmarks,

384
00:20:50,470 --> 00:20:54,010
这种想法的原理就是
and the idea behind this is to follow the animal

385
00:20:54,010 --> 00:20:57,740
动物可以发现不同标志
as it learns the location of the various landmarks

386
00:20:57,740 --> 00:21:01,710
和真正能逃脱的洞的关系。
in relation to the one true escape hole.

387
00:21:01,710 --> 00:21:05,620
弗里曼：找到出口对于章鱼来说没问题,
Freeman: The octopus has no problem finding its way out.

388
00:21:05,620 --> 00:21:09,150
实验表明当标志移动后,
Trials have shown that when the markers are moved,

389
00:21:09,150 --> 00:21:12,860
它就会朝着这个它认为代表出口的标志前进,
it will head towards what it believes to be the exit sign,

390
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:16,930
甚至这个标志已经不再位于真正洞口的上面。
even if it's no longer above the escape hole.

391
00:21:16,930 --> 00:21:22,930
很明显,这个动物可以计划、行动和记忆,
Clearly,the animal plans,acts,and remembers.

392
00:21:22,930 --> 00:21:29,540
它以较高的复杂程度进行思考,那么它有意识吗？
It's thinking at a high level of complexity,but is it conscious?

393
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:32,850
Edelman: 我定义意识为
Edelman: I define consciousness as the stitching together

394
00:21:32,850 --> 00:21:36,450
整合所有不同的感觉输入
of all of the diverse sensory input

395
00:21:36,450 --> 00:21:39,820
进入我的脑内--视觉输入,
that's coming into my brain -- visual input,

396
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:43,590
触觉结合听觉,我所听到的--
sense of touch combined with auditory,what I hear --

397
00:21:43,590 --> 00:21:47,490
将这些感觉集成一个整体
and the remembrance of all of that stuff

398
00:21:47,490 --> 00:21:50,400
并对其产生记忆。
stitched together as a unitary whole.

399
00:21:50,400 --> 00:21:54,200
弗里曼：这样拥有多重脑的生物就会是具有意识的,
Freeman: So a creature with multiple brains could be conscious,

400
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:57,000
但拥有意识与拥有自我意识
but being conscious is not the same thing

401
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:03,010
并不一样,现在我们还没有办法
as being self-aware,and right now,we have no way

402
00:22:03,010 --> 00:22:07,380
检测意识或是自我意识。
of measuring either consciousness or self awareness.

403
00:22:07,380 --> 00:22:11,580
Edelman: 如果有人问我我的狗是否有意识,
Edelman: If somebody were to ask me whether my dog is conscious,

404
00:22:11,580 --> 00:22:15,520
作为一个养宠物的人和爱狗的人,我会说,“当然,
as a pet owner and a dog lover,

405
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:17,620
“她看起来能够意识到自己的世界,
of her world.

406
00:22:17,620 --> 00:22:19,020
“她能够
“She seems to respond

407
00:22:19,020 --> 00:22:21,960
以我非常熟悉的方式回应我。”
in ways that look

408
00:22:21,960 --> 00:22:24,800
但作为一名科学家,我就必须要有
but as a scientist,I have to demand

409
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:28,170
非人动物拥有意识的证据,
evidence of consciousness in nonhuman animals

410
00:22:28,170 --> 00:22:32,240
而它们却无法告诉我它们内在的意识状态。
who cannot report to me their internal conscious states.

411
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:37,380
弗里曼：章鱼的思维还是个谜,
Freeman: The octopus's thoughts are a mystery

412
00:22:37,380 --> 00:22:40,480
或许永远都是个谜。
and may forever remain a mystery.

413
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:44,580
我们可能永远都不知道它们是否有自我意识,
We may never know if it is self-aware

414
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:46,290
它们是否会思考自己的世界。
or if it can contemplate its world.

415
00:22:46,890 --> 00:22:49,120
你能够脱离语言思考吗？
Can you think without words?

416
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:51,460
会有一种无需语言的思考方式吗？
Is there such a thing as thought without words?

417
00:22:51,460 --> 00:22:53,460
我可能只是少数的人,
Well,you know,I might be in the minority,

418
00:22:53,460 --> 00:22:55,430
但我认为也许你能够做到。
but I happen to think that you probably can.

419
00:22:55,430 --> 00:22:57,330
我认为存在视觉思维,
I think that there can be visual thoughts.

420
00:22:57,330 --> 00:22:59,430
这对于人类很难想象,
It's very hard for us as humans to imagine that

421
00:22:59,430 --> 00:23:00,900
因为...当然
because,of course,

422
00:23:00,900 --> 00:23:03,940
每当我们思考时,语言就会渗透进来。
every time we think,language breaks in.

423
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:08,680
外星语言会是什么样,
What will alien languages be like?

424
00:23:08,680 --> 00:23:12,880
和我们的语言存在共同之处吗？
Would theirs and ours have anything in common?

425
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:16,180
地球上的语言进化可能会有
The evolution of language on earth may hold the answers

426
00:23:16,180 --> 00:23:19,350
人类能否和外星人交流的答案,
to whether humans and aliens can one day communicate

427
00:23:19,350 --> 00:23:24,860
还是会因语言不通而无法交流。
or be hopelessly separated by our uncommon tongues.

428
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:30,160
人类已经进化
Human evolution has been going on

429
00:23:30,160 --> 00:23:33,100
大概五百万年了,
for roughly 5 million years,

430
00:23:33,100 --> 00:23:34,970
这在宇宙诞生的
and that's just a blip

431
00:23:34,970 --> 00:23:39,240
135亿年中只是一瞬。
in the 13.5 billion-year history of creation.

432
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:40,980
有可能
Chances are

433
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:44,410
我们不是宇宙中最聪明的生物。
we are not the smartest creatures in the universe.

434
00:23:44,410 --> 00:23:49,320
如果我们遇到了远比我们先进的外星人,
If we met aliens far more advanced than us,

435
00:23:49,320 --> 00:23:51,550
我们能否沟通？
could we communicate?

436
00:23:51,550 --> 00:23:54,490
我们能否说同样的语言？
Would we speak the same language?

437
00:23:54,490 --> 00:23:56,660
一个比我们古老一百万年的
What would language be like

438
00:23:56,660 --> 00:24:01,100
外星种族的语言会是什么样呢？
for a species a million years older than us?

439
00:24:04,900 --> 00:24:08,170
Simon Kirby教授把语言视为
Professor Simon Kirby sees language

440
00:24:08,170 --> 00:24:11,170
可以随时间演化的活物。
as a living thing that evolves over time.

441
00:24:11,170 --> 00:24:14,540
地球上的各种语言,
The sheer variety of languages on earth,

442
00:24:14,540 --> 00:24:17,080
6,000种独特的语言,
6,000 unique tongues,

443
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:19,250
反映了我们如何思考
are mirrors of how we think

444
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:22,450
以及思维在未来如何改变。
and how thought may change in the future.

445
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:26,490
现在我们做的是很杰出的事情,
Right now,we're doing something pretty extraordinary.

446
00:24:26,490 --> 00:24:28,520
讲话时我会慢慢呼出气
I'm breathing out slowly

447
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:31,830
同时像快速的芭蕾动作一样
while at the same time moving my tongue,jaw,and lips

448
00:24:31,830 --> 00:24:35,100
运动舌头、下巴和嘴唇。
in an incredibly fast ballet of movement.

449
00:24:35,100 --> 00:24:39,370
我可以通过这种形式将我脑中的想法
I can use this skill to take a thought that's in my head

450
00:24:39,370 --> 00:24:41,900
转移到你的头脑中。
and transfer it into yours.

451
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:47,510
弗里曼：Simon想要知道为什么会这样。
Freeman: Simon wants to know why this happens.

452
00:24:51,080 --> 00:24:53,380
Kirby: 我们最终如何走到
Kirby: How did we end up having a language

453
00:24:53,380 --> 00:24:55,380
使用具有独特语法结构的语言
with a particular kind of structure

454
00:24:55,380 --> 00:24:58,120
来互相交谈的呢？
that allows us to talk about anything?

455
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:02,360
令人惊讶的是,我们能够得到答案的线索,
It turns out,surprisingly,that we can get a clue to the answer

456
00:25:02,360 --> 00:25:04,890
就通过一个简单的传话筒游戏。
by looking at a simple game like broken telephone.

457
00:25:04,890 --> 00:25:07,230
在传话筒游戏中,
In the game of broken telephone,

458
00:25:07,230 --> 00:25:11,900
一句话随着一个人到另一个人演变,
a sentence evolves as it's passed from player to player,

459
00:25:11,900 --> 00:25:14,570
但生物学上并未变化。
but it doesn't evolve biologically.

460
00:25:14,570 --> 00:25:19,510
而且,它还会随着不同文化演化过程变化。
Rather,it evolves through a process of cultural evolution.

461
00:25:24,910 --> 00:25:26,580
谢谢。
Thanks.

462
00:25:26,580 --> 00:25:29,520
刚开始的句子是,
So,the original sentence was,

463
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:33,290
“生存竞争中特定文字的存在
in the struggle for existence

464
00:25:33,290 --> 00:25:34,790
是由于自然选择。”
is natural selection.”

465
00:25:34,790 --> 00:25:37,760
而你们将这句话变成了
and what you guys turned it into was,

466
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:41,200
“越长久的存在已经不能代表这种长久的存在”。
“Longer existence is not longer existence.”

467
00:25:41,200 --> 00:25:42,930
[笑]
[ laughter ]

468
00:25:42,930 --> 00:25:45,430
弗里曼：这个句子似乎已经从
Freeman: The sentence seems to have mutated

469
00:25:45,430 --> 00:25:47,300
有意义突变为无意义了,
from sense to nonsense.

470
00:25:47,300 --> 00:25:50,140
但Simon认为明显的是
But Simon argues what's significant

471
00:25:50,140 --> 00:25:53,980
句子会变得更短更容易学习。
is the sentence became shorter and easier to learn.

472
00:25:53,980 --> 00:25:57,750
这就是语言如何演变的。
That is how language evolves.

473
00:25:57,750 --> 00:25:59,880
传话筒游戏
So,the broken telephone game

474
00:25:59,880 --> 00:26:04,120
就像是微型版本的文化演变。
acts as a kind of miniature version of cultural evolution.

475
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:07,090
游戏中每个人要做的是
So what's happening is that each player in the game

476
00:26:07,090 --> 00:26:08,890
听一个句子
is hearing a sentence

477
00:26:08,890 --> 00:26:11,990
并在他们脑中简单描绘出这个句子
and mapping it somehow into their brains briefly

478
00:26:11,990 --> 00:26:14,560
接着尝试复述出来。
and then trying to reproduce it again.

479
00:26:14,560 --> 00:26:16,830
因为这个过程是不完美的,
Because that process is imperfect,

480
00:26:16,830 --> 00:26:19,070
句子就可以随时间变化,
the sentence can evolve over time,

481
00:26:19,070 --> 00:26:20,770
然后最终我们得到的句子
so the sentence we get at the end

482
00:26:20,770 --> 00:26:22,700
就和开始的句子不同了,
is different from the one at the start,

483
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:26,270
而这里所发生的就是句子以某种方式适应了
and what's happened is that that sentence has adapted in some way

484
00:26:26,270 --> 00:26:29,640
玩游戏的人的思维方式。
to the brains of the people playing the game.

485
00:26:30,750 --> 00:26:34,080
弗里曼：若要科学地证明这点很难,
Freeman: Proving this scientifically is difficult

486
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:38,250
因为语言不会留下化石记录以供研究。
because language doesn't leave a fossil record behind to study.

487
00:26:38,250 --> 00:26:40,860
Simon可以解决这个问题,
Simon tackles this problem

488
00:26:40,860 --> 00:26:44,260
通过一个字一个字的创造外星语言,
by creating alien languages word by word,

489
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:47,460
然后观察其如何适应人类大脑。
then watching them adapt to human brains.

490
00:26:47,460 --> 00:26:50,700
我们想看看是否能够在实验室
So,we wanted to see if we could re-create

491
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:54,200
重建语言的文化演化,
the cultural evolution of language in the lab,

492
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:57,510
我们研究的在几百年中发生的
whether we could study something that take hundreds of years

493
00:26:57,510 --> 00:27:01,610
可以基本上归纳于一下午的时间。
and see it happen in basically an afternoon.

494
00:27:01,610 --> 00:27:03,280
我们做的是设计一个实验,
So what we did is we set up an experiment

495
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:06,180
实验中教给志愿者
where we teach a volunteer

496
00:27:06,180 --> 00:27:09,320
我们发明的外星语言,
an alien language that we've made up

497
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:11,090
以这种方式进行测试。
and then test them on it.

498
00:27:11,090 --> 00:27:14,960
在实验中,我们有很多种外星水果,
So in the setup,we have a bunch of alien fruits that have names,

499
00:27:14,960 --> 00:27:16,760
每个名字都不同,
but every name is different.

500
00:27:16,760 --> 00:27:19,760
这种语言基本上是不可能学会的。
And this language is essentially impossible to learn.

501
00:27:19,760 --> 00:27:21,760
好的,Adam,现在请你来为我
Okay,Adam,so,now I'm gonna ask you

502
00:27:21,760 --> 00:27:24,970
试着鉴别这些外星水果。
to try and identify some of these alien fruits for me.

503
00:27:24,970 --> 00:27:27,130
Pohumo.
Pohumo.

504
00:27:27,130 --> 00:27:29,640
是这个,
That one.

505
00:27:29,640 --> 00:27:31,640
Wagahuki.
Wagahuki.

